---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import PhaseHeading from '../components/PhaseHeading.astro';
import SkillBlock from '../components/SkillBlock.astro';
import RpaFlow from '../components/RpaFlow.astro';
import DemoPlayer from '../components/DemoPlayer.astro';
import KeepGoing from '../components/KeepGoing.astro';
import { slug } from '../lib/slug';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
// the jump list under the flow strip: one row per phase heading below, a chip per block in it
const PHASES = [
  { id: 'ticket', label: 'Ticket', hue: 'text-muted', blocks: ['fetch-ticket', 'attach-to-ticket'] },
  { id: 'refine', label: 'Refine', hue: 'text-green', blocks: ['refine-ticket'] },
  { id: 'plan', label: 'Plan', hue: 'text-blue', blocks: ['create-implementation-plan', 'harden-artifact', 'split-plan-tasks'] },
  { id: 'act', label: 'Act', hue: 'text-orange', blocks: ['Execute the plan', 'execute-plan-tasks'] },
  { id: 'consolidate', label: 'Consolidate', hue: 'text-purple', blocks: ['fresh-eyes-review', 'handover'] },
  {
    id: 'extra', label: 'Any time', hue: 'text-muted',
    blocks: ['create-manual-test-instructions', 'review-ticket', 'verify-understanding',
      'check-ticket-implementation', 'grill-me', 'prepare-prompt', 'plans-directory'],
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Task workflow · agent-toolkit"
  description="Refine, plan, act, consolidate: turn a ticket into requirements, a plan, then reviewed code, with a clean handoff at every step."
  back
>
  <PageHeader title="Task workflow skills" heading="Refine, Plan, Act, Consolidate">
    <p class="text-muted mb-3">
      The <b>Task workflow</b> skills are a set of utilities that support any developer through a
      standard development task, from fetching the ticket to handing the finished code over to
      review.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted">
      They are based on the <a class="more-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        href="https://medium.com/engineering-in-the-age-of-ai/the-refine-plan-act-pattern-for-agentic-ai-coding-59ee013e4427">RPAC
        pattern</a>: Refine, Plan, Act, Consolidate. Misunderstandings surface in a reviewable
      document before any code is written, and a failed attempt costs a retry from the last file,
      not the whole task.
    </p>
    <RpaFlow />
    <p class="text-muted">
      Each phase runs in a fresh session and hands over a file, not chat history, so the context
      stays sharp.
    </p>
    <nav aria-label="Skills on this page" class="mt-8">
      <div class="text-muted text-[13px] mb-3">On this page</div>
      <div class="grid gap-x-6 gap-y-2.5 md:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] items-baseline">
        {PHASES.map((phase) => (
          <>
            <a href={`#${phase.id}`}
              class:list={['font-mono text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] no-underline hover:underline', phase.hue]}>
              {phase.label}</a>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
              {phase.blocks.map((name) => (
                <a href={`#${slug(name)}`}
                  class="bg-panel2 border border-border rounded-md px-2.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[12px] text-text no-underline hover:border-muted">
                  {name}</a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </>
        ))}
      </div>
    </nav>
  </PageHeader>
  <PhaseHeading id="ticket" kicker="Ticket" color="muted" heading="Start from the ticket" />
  <SkillBlock name="fetch-ticket" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Every task starts with a ticket. This skill downloads it from Jira, GitHub, Azure DevOps or
      similar into a self-contained <b class="sk">TICKET.md</b>, attachments and linked tickets
      included. No tracker? Write the file by hand and the workflow stays the same. An attachment
      it can't download gets flagged, so you can add it with <b class="sk">/attach-to-ticket</b>.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="08-fetch-ticket" cols={72} rows={9} label="fetch-ticket downloading a ticket into a self-contained file" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="attach-to-ticket" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted">
      Some attachments never make it through the fetch: a screenshot from a chat, a file behind a
      login. Paste it, or give its path or link, and the agent saves it next to
      <b class="sk">TICKET.md</b> and adds it to the ticket file with a short caption, so the next
      session sees it too. It asks before filling a missing attachment or writing a caption. It
      only touches your local files: your tracker stays untouched.
    </p>
  </SkillBlock>
  <PhaseHeading id="refine" kicker="Refine" color="green" heading="Agree on what to build" />
  <SkillBlock name="refine-ticket" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Defines the <b>WHAT</b>. The agent checks the ticket against the actual codebase and
      interviews you, one question at a time, each with a recommended answer. No silent
      assumptions: you decide. The result is a validated <b class="sk">REQUIREMENTS.md</b>. No
      ticket yet? Give it a raw idea and it grills you on the goal and the scope first.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="02-refine-ticket" cols={72} rows={13} label="refine-ticket asking a grilling question" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <PhaseHeading id="plan" kicker="Plan" color="blue" heading="Decide how to build it" />
  <SkillBlock name="create-implementation-plan" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Defines the <b>HOW</b>. The agent studies the code, settles the technical decisions with you
      and writes a self-contained <b class="sk">PLAN.md</b> that a fresh session can execute step
      by step.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="09-create-plan" cols={72} rows={13} label="create-implementation-plan asking where the serializer should live" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="harden-artifact" color="green" level={3}
    link={`${base}/fresh-eyes-review/`} linkLabel="How the fresh eyes review works">
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      An optional check between a document and the phase that builds on it. Run it on a
      <b class="sk">REQUIREMENTS.md</b> or a <b class="sk">PLAN.md</b> in a fresh session, not
      the one that wrote it: a sub-agent with a clean context checks it against the ticket, the
      code and the related tickets, and your session tries to disprove each finding. You decide
      the ones that survive, one at a time, each with a recommendation: fix, dismiss or defer.
      When the document is ready, you get the commands that start the next phase.
      <b class="sk">/refine-ticket</b> and <b class="sk">/create-implementation-plan</b> both
      offer it when they finish.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="24-harden-artifact" cols={72} rows={21} label="harden-artifact fixing a proven gap in the plan, then handing off the next phase" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="split-plan-tasks" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      A plan that lands in one sweep is hard to review. Give this skill the
      <b class="sk">PLAN.md</b> and it splits the steps into small tasks, each one leaving the
      project building and its tests passing, grouped into PR-sized batches. The first batch gives
      you something to click or run as early as the plan allows. You approve the breakdown, then
      it appends the task list to the plan, with a done check and a suggested commit subject per
      task. The plan's own text stays as it was.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="22-split-plan-tasks" cols={72} rows={14} label="split-plan-tasks settling a task boundary, then appending the task list to the plan" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <PhaseHeading id="act" kicker="Act" color="orange" heading="Build it">
    <p class="text-muted mt-3">
      Run the whole plan with a plain prompt, or one task at a time when you split it.
    </p>
  </PhaseHeading>
  <SkillBlock name="Execute the plan" color="text" github={false} level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Running the whole plan needs no skill at all. Open a fresh session and ask the agent to
      execute the plan: everything it needs is in <b class="sk">PLAN.md</b>. Ask it to run your
      project's checks too, so it verifies its own work.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="10-execute-plan" cols={72} rows={7} label="a plain prompt executing the plan and running the checks" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="execute-plan-tasks" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Split the plan into tasks? Then run this instead, one task per fresh session. It implements
      that task and nothing beyond it, runs the task's done check and your project's checks, ticks
      the task off in <b class="sk">PLAN.md</b> and stops, so you review and commit before the
      next one. A deviation from the plan it had to settle on the way goes into a
      <b class="sk">DECISIONS.md</b> next to the plan, with its why. Every run ends with the command
      for the next task, and tells you when a group is complete and ready to become a PR. It never
      commits or pushes.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="23-execute-plan-tasks" cols={72} rows={12} label="execute-plan-tasks implementing one task, ticking it off and handing off the next" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <PhaseHeading id="consolidate" kicker="Consolidate" color="purple" heading="Review and hand over" />
  <SkillBlock name="fresh-eyes-review" color="pink" github={false} level={3}
    link={`${base}/fresh-eyes-review/`} linkLabel="See the fresh eyes review">
    <p class="text-muted">
      First step of <b>Consolidate</b>: before handing over, let a sub-agent with a clean context
      review the changeset. It catches what the session that wrote the code misses.
    </p>
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="handover" color="green" level={3}
    article="https://medium.com/engineering-in-the-age-of-ai/the-missing-step-in-agentic-coding-the-handover-d1963c3d2c1d"
    articleLabel="Read more about handover">
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Closes the task. Give it a ticket id, or nothing at all, and it finds the task's planning
      files, gathers the decisions made along the way, from the ticket to the session itself,
      matches the plan against the actual diff and writes a <b class="sk">HANDOVER.md</b>: a
      paste-ready PR description with the decisions worth knowing, where to look and what's
      still open, so reviewers never reconstruct intent from the diff. Your repo has a PR
      template? It fills that in instead, ticking only the boxes it can back up.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="11-handover" cols={72} rows={14} label="handover flagging an unexplained deviation, then saving a paste-ready PR description" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <PhaseHeading id="extra" kicker="Any time" color="muted" heading="Extra workflow skills">
    <p class="text-muted mt-3">Not part of the main flow, but handy when the task calls for them.</p>
  </PhaseHeading>
  <SkillBlock name="create-manual-test-instructions" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Turns a ticket or its requirements into a concise <b class="sk">MANUAL-TEST.md</b> a
      non-author can follow: what changed, how to get there, before vs after, and what to verify.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="12-manual-test" cols={72} rows={8} label="create-manual-test-instructions saving a manual test file" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="review-ticket" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      A triage glance before anyone picks a ticket up: compares it against the codebase and
      reports whether it's ready, plus the questions worth asking whoever owns the requirements.
      Each question survived an adversarial hunt for its answer across code, tracker, designs and
      docs, so you're never asked what the sources could have answered. A gap that is another
      team's or another repo's to fix comes back as a handoff, with a message ready to send to its
      owner. Verdict and briefing land in a <b class="sk">TICKET-REVIEW.md</b> next to the ticket,
      and /refine-ticket picks it up later, starting from whether those questions got an answer.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="13-review-ticket" cols={72} rows={11} label="review-ticket delivering its triage verdict" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="verify-understanding" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      The other skills write files for the next agent session. This one is for you. Run it on the
      <b class="sk">TICKET-REVIEW.md</b>, right after the review or days later just before you
      start coding. It asks you to explain the feature in your own words: who uses it, what they
      see and do, why it exists, and what each ticket adds. Then it digs into one weak spot at a
      time and corrects you using only the review and the tickets, saying so when they don't
      settle something instead of making up an answer. It plays the part of that last chat with
      your product owner before development starts. It writes no files: the conversation is the
      point.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="21-verify-understanding" cols={72} rows={19} label="verify-understanding correcting a teach-back from the ticket review" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="check-ticket-implementation" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted mb-3">
      Answers one question: how much of this ticket is already built? It splits the ticket into
      individual requirements and judges each one against the code, so the verdict lands per
      requirement instead of on the ticket as a whole: done, partially done, not done, or not
      verifiable from the code, each with a short note and the <b class="sk">file:line</b> where it
      was checked.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      The report goes into a <b class="sk">TICKET-STATUS.md</b>, headed by the tally and the
      requirements that need attention. Useful when you pick up a branch someone else started, or
      before you call a ticket finished. Give it a ticket link and it fetches the ticket first.
      Apart from that, the report is the only file it writes: your code and the ticket stay
      untouched.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="18-check-ticket" cols={72} rows={12} label="check-ticket-implementation reporting a verdict per requirement" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="grill-me" color="green" level={3} article="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills"
    articleLabel="See the original by Matt Pocock">
    <p class="text-muted mb-3">
      The interview that refine-ticket and create-implementation-plan run, on its own. Bring any
      plan, design or idea and say <b class="sk">grill me</b>: the agent asks one question at a
      time, each with its recommended answer, until every branch is settled. What the code can
      answer, it looks up instead of asking you. Want a REQUIREMENTS.md at the end? Use
      refine-ticket instead.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted">
      This skill was originally created by
      <a class="more-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/mattpocock">Matt Pocock</a>.
      We made it available in this toolkit with one small edit: it words its questions with
      <a class="more-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        href="https://github.com/eai-org/agent-toolkit/blob/main/skills/explain-in-simple-language/SKILL.md">/explain-in-simple-language</a>,
      so they are easy to follow.
    </p>
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="prepare-prompt" color="green" level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted">
      Every phase above ends with a ready command for the next session. For anything else there's
      this skill: when a fresh session should continue the work, check it, or pick up what was
      left open, it writes the prompt to paste there. It keeps only what the next session can't
      find on its own, points at files instead of pasting them, and checks every path and command
      it prints. Asking a new session to check the work? It leaves out its own conclusions, so
      they can't steer the check.
    </p>
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="plans-directory" color="green" rule level={3}>
    <p class="text-muted">
      Opt-in rule for where planning documents go: one folder per task in the project's planning
      directory, with file names that say what each file is. The default is
      <b class="sk">.agents/plans/</b>, so every planning file the demos on this page save, like
      <b class="sk">1234-users.TICKET.md</b> or <b class="sk">1234-users.HANDOVER.md</b>, lands in
      <b class="sk">.agents/plans/1234-users/</b>. Most skills on this page already save their
      files this way, and with the rule installed, <b class="sk">/fetch-ticket</b> no longer has
      to ask you where your plans live. The agent also follows it for any other planning document
      it writes, like a design note or an investigation. If your project already has a planning
      directory of its own, that one wins.
      <code class="font-mono text-[12px] bg-panel2 border border-border rounded px-1.5 whitespace-nowrap">./install-opinionated-rules.sh</code>
      installs it along with all the other rules, or you can pick only the ones you want.
    </p>
  </SkillBlock>
  <section class="px-6">
    <div class="max-w-page mx-auto border-t border-border py-8">
      <a class="more-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
        href="https://medium.com/engineering-in-the-age-of-ai/how-i-use-ai-agents-to-solve-programming-tasks-daily-2a68a5828b8e">
        Read more about the task workflow &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <KeepGoing current="task-workflow" />
</Base>
